    "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A circle has a diameter of <math alttext=\"12.6\"><mn>12.6</mn></math> centimeters. What is the radius of the circle, in centimeters?</p>",
    "type": "grid-in",
    "correctAnswer": "6.3, 63/10",
    "explanation": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6.3\"><mn>6.3</mn></math>. The diameter of a circle is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 r\"><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> represents the radius of the circle. It&rsquo;s given that a circle has a diameter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"12.6\"><mn>12.6</mn></math> centimeters. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"12.6 equals 2 r\"><mn>12.6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6.3 equals r\"><mn>6.3</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></math>. It follows that the radius of the circle, in centimeters, is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6.3\"><mn>6.3</mn></math>. Note that 6.3 and 63/10 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>"
